<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Archelaus I of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 413–<a href="/years/399-bce">399 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Archelaus I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> from about 413 to 399 BCE. He built roads and fortresses, strengthened the army and moved the royal seat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aegae</a> to Pella. A patron of Greek culture, he drew the dramatist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> and the painter Zeuxis to his court. He was killed in 399 BCE, and years of instability followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archelaus-i-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Archelaus I of Macedon" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Didrachm_of_Archelaos_I_King_of_Macedonia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Archelaus was the son of Perdiccas II and his wife Simache, who is thought to have been once enslaved by Archelaus' uncle, Alcetas. Plato, through his interlocutors in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgias">Gorgias</a>, wrote that Archelaus murdered both his uncle Alcetas and his unnamed seven-year-old half-brother to gain the throne, but this cannot be confirmed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> reports that he gave his eldest daughter to the king of Elimea during a war against the Lyncestae. His son Orestes later became king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Soon after taking power Archelaus was able to reverse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>'s relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which had been a major threat for half a century. After the Athenian defeat at Syracuse in late 413 BCE destroyed most of their ships, Athens needed a large amount of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and Archelaus, in a position to set the price, supplied it generously. The Athenians honoured him and his children with the titles of proxenos and euergetes.</p><p>He issued an abundance of good quality coinage, built strongholds, cut straight roads, important for moving the army, and improved the organisation of the cavalry and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> infantry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> credited him with doing more for his kingdom's military infrastructure than all his predecessors together.</p><p>In his new palace at Pella, to which he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aegae</a>, he hosted poets and tragedians, among them Agathon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>, who wrote Archelaus and The Bacchae while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and the painter Zeuxis. He reorganised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a> at Dion, a festival of musical and athletic competitions honouring Olympian Zeus and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to Aelian, Archelaus was killed in 399 BCE during a hunt by one of the royal pages, Crateuas. Constantine Paparrigopoulos names three accomplices, among them Decamnichos, who had insulted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a> in front of the king, was flogged in punishment with Archelaus' permission, and remained at court. Other sources give other versions of the king's death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Archelaus of Macedon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Macedonia. Died in Macedonia. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
